# infrastructure/tailscale/sealed-operator-oauth-secret.yaml.example
#
# Prerequisites — in the Tailscale admin console:
#
# 1. Add the following to your Access Controls (ACL) under tagOwners:
#
#      "tagOwners": {
#        "tag:k8s-operator": [],
#        "tag:k8s": ["tag:k8s-operator"]
#      }
#
#    tag:k8s-operator  →  assigned to the operator itself; only tailnet admins can assign it
#    tag:k8s           →  assigned to connector/proxy devices managed by the operator;
#                         owned by tag:k8s-operator so the operator can manage these devices
#
# 2. Create an OAuth client at: Admin Console → Settings → OAuth clients (Trust credentials)
#    Scopes — all scoped to tag:k8s-operator:
#      - General / Services   → Write
#      - Devices / Core       → Write
#      - Keys / Auth Keys     → Write
#
#    Note the client_id and client_secret values after creation (client_secret is shown only once).
#
# 3. Generate and seal the secret (no trailing newline risk with --from-literal):
#
#    kubectl create secret generic operator-oauth \
#      --namespace=tailscale \
#      --from-literal=client_id='<YOUR_CLIENT_ID>' \
#      --from-literal=client_secret='<YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET>' \
#      --dry-run=client -o yaml | \
#      kubeseal --format yaml > infrastructure/tailscale/sealed-operator-oauth.yaml
#
# The operator-oauth Secret must exist in the tailscale namespace before the operator pod starts.
# The SealedSecret controller creates it automatically when the SealedSecret is applied.